    "evidence_text": "In this paper, we propose a neural based Nepali NER using latest state-of-the-art architecture based on grapheme-level which doesn't require any hand-crafted features and no data pre-processing.\n\nRecent neural architecture like  is used to relax the need to hand-craft the features and need to use part-of-speech tag to determine the category of the entity.\n\nHowever, this architecture have been studied for languages like English, and German and not been applied to languages like Nepali which is a low resource language i.e limited data set to train the model.\n\nTraditional methods like Hidden Markov Model (HMM) with rule based approaches,, and Support Vector Machine (SVM) with manual feature-engineering have been applied but they perform poor compared to neural.\n\nTherefore, we created the named entity annotated dataset partly with the help of Dataturk to train a neural model.\n\nTo best of our knowledge we are the first\n\nResearchers have shown that the neural networks like CNN, RNN, LSTM, GRU can capture the semantic knowledge of language better with the help of pre-trained embbeddings like word2vec, glove or fasttext.\n\nFirst we are comparing BiLSTM, BiLSTM+CNN, BiLSTM+CRF, BiLSTM+CNN+CRF models with CNN model and Stanford CRF model.\n\nSecondly, we show the comparison between models trained on general word embeddings, word embedding + character-level embedding, word embedding + part-of-speech(POS) one-hot encoding and word embedding + grapheme clustered or sub-word embedding.\n\nThis trained model consists of vectors for 72782 unique words.\n\nApproach ::: Features ::: Character-level embeddings\t and  successfully presented that the character-level embeddings, extracted using CNN, when combined with word embeddings enhances the NER model performance significantly, as it is able to capture morphological features of a word.\n\nFigure FIGREF7 shows the grapheme-level CNN used in our model, where inputs to CNN are graphemes.\n\nwere removed.\n\nIn order to label our dataset with POS-tags, we first created POS annotated dataset of 6946 sentences and 16225 unique words extracted from POS-tagged Nepali National Corpus and trained a BiLSTM model with 95.14% accuracy which was used to create POS-tags for our dataset.\n\nWe trained our model with shuffled training set using Adam optimizer with hyper-parameters mentioned in table TABREF30.\n\nAll our models were trained on single layer of LSTM network.\n\nWe found out Adam was giving better performance and faster convergence compared to Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD).\n\nExperiments ::: Tagging Scheme\tCurrently, for our experiments we trained our model on IO (Inside, Outside) format for both the dataset, hence the dataset does not contain any B-type annotation unlike in BIO (Beginning, Inside, Outside) scheme.\n\nExperiments ::: Hyper-parameters Tuning\tWe ran our experiment looking for the best hyper-parameters by changing learning rate from (0,1, 0.01, 0.001, 0.0001), weight decay from [$10^{-1}$, $10^{-2}$, $10^{-3}$, $10^{-4}$, $10^{-5}$, $10^{-6}$, $10^{-7}$], batch size from [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128], hidden\n\nsize from [8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512 1024].\n\nEvaluation\tIn this section, we present the details regarding evaluation and comparison of our models with other baselines.\n\nWe can deduce that Skip Gram models perform better compared CBOW models for word2vec and fasttext.\n\nHere,\n\nFrom this table TABREF25, we can clearly observe that model using fastText_Skip Gram embeddings outperforms all other models.\n\nTable TABREF35 shows the model architecture comparison between all the models experimented.\n\nThis model is trained till the current function value is less than $1\\mathrm {e}{-2}$.\n\nAll models are evaluated using CoNLL-2003 evaluation script to calculate entity-wise precision, recall and f1 score.\n\nDiscussion\tIn this paper we present that we can exploit the power of neural network to train the model to perform downstream NLP tasks like Named Entity Recognition even in Nepali language.\n\nWe showed that the word vectors learned through fasttext skip gram model performs better than other word embedding because of its capability to represent sub-word\n\nWe also found out that stemming post-positions can help a lot in improving model performance because of inflectional characteristics of Nepali language.\n\nWe also present a neural architecture BiLSTM+CNN(grapheme-level) which turns out to be performing on par with BiLSTM+CNN(character-level) under the same configuration.",
